  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Calculate the points where the hyperbola \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\nintersects the line $y=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=1,$ we substitute $y=1$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the horizontal line $y=1$ at the points $(\\pm 3,1).$",
